---
import LessonLayout from '../../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
const lessonRef = "CCC 2759-2802";
const lessonScripture = "Mt 6:9-13; Lk 11:1-4; Rom 8:15; Gal 4:6";
---
<LessonLayout title="The Our Father" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

  <section class="content">

    <h2>Big Question</h2>

    <p class="big-question">If God, the creator of the universe, said to you, "Call me Dad," what would you feel? Embarrassment, trust, tenderness, disbelief?</p>

    <p>
      The disciples watched Jesus pray. They saw him withdraw before dawn, spend long hours on the mountain, speaking to someone he called "Abba." Something about that intimacy stirred in them a mix of awe and envy. They prayed — they prayed the psalms, they prayed in the synagogue, they prayed like every faithful Jew — but they had never seen anyone pray like this, with such freedom, such tenderness. Until one day they dared to ask: "Lord, teach us to pray" (Lk 11:1). And Jesus did not give them a theory. He gave them words. He gave them the Our Father. Tertullian, in the third century, called it the <em>breviarium totius Evangelii</em>: the summary of the whole Gospel. St. Augustine said: "Run through all the prayers that are in Scripture, and I do not think you can find anything that is not contained in the Lord's Prayer." Every word has been measured by the very Son of God. It is not one prayer among many: it is <em>the</em> prayer.
    </p>
    <p>
      To pray the Our Father is to sit in the school of Jesus. It is to let him take us by the hand and lead us to the Father. It is to learn, word by word, how to be children. Because the hardest thing about Christianity is not believing that God exists: it is believing that he is Father.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content">

    <h2>What Is the Lord's Prayer?</h2>

    <p>
      The Lord's Prayer occupies a unique place in Christian prayer. It is the only prayer Jesus himself taught us. The Catechism devotes the entirety of Part Four, from paragraph 2759 through 2865, to commenting on this prayer phrase by phrase. No other prayer in the Church receives such treatment.
    </p>

    <p>
      "The Lord's Prayer is truly the summary of the whole Gospel" (CCC 2761). The Our Father contains everything we need to ask for and everything we need to believe. The structure is luminous: an initial invocation ("Our Father, who art in heaven") followed by seven petitions. The first three concern God: his name, his kingdom, his will. The last four concern us: bread, forgiveness, the test, evil. It is a perfect balance between theology and concrete life.
    </p>

    <p>
      The prayer has two versions in the New Testament: Matthew's (Mt 6:9-13) and Luke's (Lk 11:2-4). Matthew's, more extensive, is the one adopted by the Church's liturgy. It has seven petitions, the biblical number of fullness. Luke's has five. Both agree on the essential point: God is Father, and we can speak with him with the trust of a child.
    </p>

    <div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
      <h4 class="depth-box__title">Activity</h4>
      <p>Take a moment and pray the Our Father out loud, very slowly. Do not recite it from memory. Read each word as if it were the first time you heard it. Pause after each phrase. Which word strikes you today? Which one do you resist saying sincerely? Write it down.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>The Prayer of the New Covenant</h3>

    <p>
      The Our Father is not simply a beautiful prayer. It is the prayer of the New Covenant. Moses gave the people the Law; Jesus gives us the prayer. The Law said what must be done; the Our Father says who we are: children. It is a prayer that can only be prayed fully by those who have received the Spirit of filial adoption. St. Paul explains it in Romans: "You have received a spirit of adoption as sons, by which we cry, 'Abba! Father!'" (Rom 8:15). And in Galatians: "And because you are sons, God has sent the Spirit of his Son into our hearts, crying, 'Abba! Father!'" (Gal 4:6). To pray the Our Father is to let the Holy Spirit pray within us.
    </p>

    <div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
      <h4 class="depth-box__title">Connection</h4>
      <p>Do you pray the Our Father attentively or on autopilot? The next time you participate in Mass, when the Our Father comes, make an internal pause. Do not follow the crowd. Pronounce each word consciously. You will notice the difference.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">

    <h2>"Our Father": Abba, Trust, and Community</h2>

    <p>
      The most revolutionary word in the Our Father is the first one: "Father." Jesus did not teach us to call God "Almighty," "Most High," or "Creator" — though God is all of those — but "Father." In Aramaic, the language Jesus spoke, the word is <em>Abba</em>. It is not exactly "Daddy" in the childish sense, but neither is it a cold and distant word. It was the term an adult son used to address his father with both respect and affection. It is the word of family intimacy.
    </p>

    <p>
      "We can invoke God as 'Father' because he has been revealed to us by his Son made man and because his Spirit makes him known to us" (CCC 2780). Calling God "Father" is not projecting onto him our human experiences of fatherhood, sometimes beautiful and sometimes painful. It is the reverse: all human fatherhood is an imperfect reflection of God's fatherhood. St. Paul says that God is the Father "from whom every family in heaven and on earth is named" (Eph 3:15). If your experience with your earthly father was difficult or absent, know that the Our Father is also for you. God is not like the fathers who fail. He is the Father who heals the wounds other fathers caused.
    </p>

    <h3>Not "My Father" but "<em>Our</em> Father"</h3>

    <p>
      The plural is no accident. Jesus did not say "My Father" but "Our Father." "If it is ours, it is common to all; if it is common, it belongs to others as much as to me" (St. John Chrysostom). In saying "our," I acknowledge that God is no one's private property. He is not "my" God against "your" God. He is the Father of all the baptized, and in some sense, of all humanity. Praying "Our Father" is a communal act even when I pray alone. The Church is present in every Our Father. That is why it is the Christian's prayer in every circumstance: at Mass, in the rosary, at the bedside of the sick, in a trench, in joy and in fear.
    </p>

    <div class="depth-box depth-box--raiz">
      <h4 class="depth-box__title">Context</h4>
      <p>In the Old Testament, God is called Father of the people of Israel on rare occasions (Dt 32:6; Is 63:16; Jer 3:19), but always with reserve: the covenant relationship was more that of a servant with his Lord. What is radically new in the Gospel is that Jesus, the eternal Son, makes us sharers in his own filial relationship with the Father. We are no longer servants: we are sons in the Son. The Our Father is born from the Trinitarian heart of the Christian faith.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">

    <h2>"Who Art in Heaven": The Majesty of God</h2>

    <p>
      The second part of the invocation seems to distance God just when we have drawn him close: we have just called him "Father" and immediately we say "who art in heaven." Is this not contradictory? The Catechism explains that this is not about a place. "Heaven is not a place, but a way of being of God" (CCC 2794). When we say God is "in heaven," we do not locate him in the clouds; we acknowledge his transcendence, his majesty, his radical difference from all created things. God is Father, yes, but he is not a buddy. He is not a celestial "pal." He is the Father who deserves all reverence.
    </p>

    <p>
      "The expression 'who art in heaven' does not designate a place, but the majesty of God" (CCC 2794). Heaven is the horizon of fullness, the goal toward which we walk. To say that God is in heaven is to say that God is our definitive homeland. The invocation of the Our Father contains in seed the whole of Christian hope: we have a Father who loves us and that Father awaits us in his home. The whole prayer is a journey from the "heaven" where he is to the "heaven" where we hope to be.
    </p>

    <div class="depth-box depth-box--arbol">
      <h4 class="depth-box__title">Deepening</h4>
      <p>Fulton Sheen, the great Catholic communicator of the twentieth century, devoted several memorable reflections to the Our Father. He said that the Our Father is the only prayer that, if truly prayed, would change the world. He pointed out that the first word is "Father" because God's fatherhood is the foundation of everything else: if God is Father, then we are brothers and sisters, and fraternity is not an option but a consequence. His book <em>Life of Christ</em> contains a commentary on the Our Father that remains timely.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">

    <h2>Biblical Connection: "Lord, Teach Us to Pray"</h2>

    <blockquote>
      <p>He was praying in a certain place, and when he ceased, one of his disciples said to him, "Lord, teach us to pray, as John taught his disciples." And he said to them, "When you pray, say: Father, hallowed be thy name. Thy kingdom come. Give us each day our daily bread; and forgive us our sins, for we ourselves forgive everyone who is indebted to us; and lead us not into temptation."</p>
      <cite>Luke 11:1-4</cite>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Prayer is born of a humble request: "teach us." The disciples do not assume they know how to pray. They recognize they need to learn. Jesus responds with precise, simple, memorable words. He does not give them a meditation method or a relaxation technique. He gives them words to address God. And the first one is "Father." All of Christianity is contained in that leap: from the distant God to the near God; from fear to trust; from servant to son. The Bible ends up calling God "Abba" because Jesus has drawn us inside his own relationship with the Father.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content">

    <h2>For Reflection</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>When you say "Our Father," who are you including? Is there anyone you struggle to include as a brother or sister?</li>
      <li>If your image of God is marked by your experience with your human father, how are they alike and how are they different?</li>
      <li>Jesus taught us to call God "Abba." Do you pray with the trust of a child or the fear of a servant?</li>
      <li>The Our Father is prayed in community. What does it mean for you to recite it at Mass alongside people you do not know?</li>
      <li>If you prayed the Our Father today as if it were the first time, what would change in how you live it?</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="content">

    <h2>Key Idea</h2>
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>Key Idea:</strong> The Our Father is Jesus's gift: the prayer that sums up the whole Gospel, where we learn to call God "Abba" with the trust of a child and the humility of one who knows that God is our heaven.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">

    <h2>Cultural Richness</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>The Gregorian <em>Pater Noster</em></strong> &mdash; The Gregorian chant of the Our Father is one of the most solemn pieces in the liturgical repertoire. In the Roman liturgy, the priest sings the introduction ("At the Savior's command...") and the people respond with the entire prayer sung. The melody, simple yet majestic, underscores the passage from human petition to filial trust. Hearing it in a Benedictine abbey is an experience that unites centuries of faith in a single voice.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Fulton Sheen and <em>Life of Christ</em></strong> &mdash; Archbishop Fulton J. Sheen (1895-1979), a pioneer of Catholic television, devoted luminous pages to the Our Father. In his book <em>Life of Christ</em> (1954), he comments on each phrase with the depth of a theologian and the warmth of a pastor. He notes that the Our Father begins with "Father" and ends with "Amen": from trust to certainty. His direct style and ability to connect with modern men and women made him famous; his television program <em>Life is Worth Living</em> reached thirty million viewers.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Part Four of the Catechism</strong> &mdash; The Catechism of the Catholic Church reserves its entire fourth part for Christian prayer (CCC 2558-2865). Within it, Section Two is an exhaustive commentary on the Our Father, article by article (CCC 2759-2865). This structure reveals the importance of this prayer: it is not an appendix but the culmination of everything the Catechism teaches. Prayer is the place where the Creed (what we believe), the sacraments (what we celebrate), and the moral life (what we live) unite in a dialogue with God.
    </p>
  </section>

</LessonLayout>
